WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.900
PIM settings are defined per role.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:05.186
Each role's settings are
independent of every other role's,

3
00:00:05.186 --> 00:00:07.650
and every assignment of
a role follows them.

4
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:11.913
The program sets them by tier,
from Chapter 04's tier model.

5
00:00:11.913 --> 00:00:15.356
Tier 0 is the control plane:
Global, Privileged Role,

6
00:00:15.356 --> 00:00:20.687
Privileged Authentication, Security
and Conditional Access Administrator.

7
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:26.503
Four-hour activations, authentication context
c1, justification and ticket required,

8
00:00:26.503 --> 00:00:29.007
approval by the Tier 0 approver group,

9
00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:32.238
and eligibility that
expires after ninety days.

10
00:00:32.238 --> 00:00:35.872
Tier 1, the management plane,
such as Exchange, SharePoint,

11
00:00:35.872 --> 00:00:39.507
Intune and Application
Administrator, gets eight hours,

12
00:00:39.507 --> 00:00:42.987
the Tier 1 approver group,
and a hundred and eighty days.

13
00:00:42.987 --> 00:00:47.750
Tier 2, scoped operations inside
administrative units, gets eight hours,

14
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.274
and approval is optional, with a
documented risk acceptance per role.

15
00:00:52.274 --> 00:00:54.478
Read-only roles need no approval,

16
00:00:54.478 --> 00:00:59.375
but still pass c1 with a justification,
and their eligibility runs a year.

17
00:00:59.375 --> 00:01:03.775
Now, what the controls really do,
because the old guides got several wrong.

18
00:01:03.775 --> 00:01:07.418
The activation maximum runs
from one to twenty-four hours,

19
00:01:07.418 --> 00:01:11.288
and it's the exposure window, so
set the shortest the work needs.

20
00:01:11.288 --> 00:01:16.045
Require MFA on activation may not
prompt at all if the user authenticated

21
00:01:16.045 --> 00:01:20.725
strongly earlier in the session, so
it proves nothing at activation time.

22
00:01:20.725 --> 00:01:24.916
That's why every role uses the
authentication context instead, which is

23
00:01:24.916 --> 00:01:29.675
Microsoft's recommendation when the goal
is authentication during activation.

24
00:01:29.675 --> 00:01:34.542
Ticket information is recorded but never
checked against a ticketing system;

25
00:01:34.542 --> 00:01:37.537
enforced correlation
belongs in the SIEM or ITSM.

26
00:01:37.537 --> 00:01:41.300
And approval needs at least one
approver, with two recommended.

27
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.372
The assignment controls close the
side door, so a well-built gate can't

28
00:01:45.372 --> 00:01:49.587
be bypassed by simply granting
someone a permanent active assignment.

29
00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:51.938
No permanent eligible assignments.

30
00:01:51.938 --> 00:01:57.050
No permanent active ones, except Global
Administrator for the emergency accounts.

31
00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:01.649
And MFA and justification are required
when an active assignment is made,

32
00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:06.840
because PIM can't apply an activation
requirement to someone who's already active.
